  "markdown_report": "The best first experiment is a **mask-safe recent-history usefulness probe** for the supplied BERT4Rec. At 0% progress, establishing trustworthy diagnostics is more useful than adding architecture complexity. No improvement is claimed before measurement.\n\n**Insights from the starting point**\n\n1. Training-position margins do not test appended-mask inference. A separate next-item probe must reproduce inference exactly.\n2. Feedback suggests broad relevance can obscure specific recent interests. It does not establish that recency weighting will improve validation HR@10.\n3. Missing item identity prevents preference assessment. Unknown compatibility, ownership and intent must remain unknown.\n\n**Insights from related work**\n\n1. BERT4Rec predicts masked items using bidirectional context [1]. Diagnostic targets must therefore remain outside visible inputs.\n2. Counterfactual history edits expose recommendation dependence [2]. Dependence alone does not establish useful personalization.\n3. Sampled ranking metrics can change model comparisons [3]. The pinned host evaluator must remain the selection authority.\n\n**Directions and framework**\n\n| Direction | Existing measurement | Proposed addition | Interpretation limit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Inference correctness | Training margins | Appended-mask and padding checks | Correctness does not establish quality |\n| History usefulness | No interventions | Recent versus older/random edits | Sensitivity alone is insufficient |\n| Representation health | Mean cosine | Centered effective rank [4] | Association does not establish causation |\n\n**Three atomic ideas**\n\nScores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Mask-safe history usefulness probe — 5/8/4.** Originality: combines family-specific controls; intervention methods already exist. Potential: distinguishes harmful instability from useful dependence; prefix holdouts imperfectly represent validation. Difficulty: reuses inference hooks; maintaining identical positions and eligibility requires care.\n- **Representation spectrum audit — 3/6/2.** Originality: adds spectral analysis to current diagnostics; effective rank is established. Potential: detects failures missed by mean cosine; causal relevance remains uncertain. Difficulty: requires only sampled embedding SVD; sampling and centering affect estimates.\n- **Mixed terminal-mask training — 3/7/4.** Originality: mixes terminal and internal masking; closely follows established masked training. Potential: may reduce inference mismatch; may weaken broader contextual learning. Difficulty: mainly changes sampling; requires another budgeted training experiment.\n\n**Selected algorithm**\n\nUse deterministic training-prefix holdouts, withholding each target completely. Compare unchanged histories with recent deletions, equally sized older/random deletions, and a length-preserving recent permutation. Repeat random controls. All branches use identical candidate IDs and eligibility masks, identical truncation rules, left padding and an appended reserved mask [1,2].\n\nMeasure signed target-rank changes, top-10 overlap and standardized target-versus-tenth-negative margins. Separate baseline-correct and baseline-incorrect cases; bootstrap paired user differences within categories and macro-average them. Rank deterioration relative to controls supports useful dependence, without establishing present intent.\n\nRetain the existing diagnostic API and measured metric definitions. Run the probe on one fixed trained checkpoint per category; preserve routing, seeds, training limits and the independently evaluated predictor. Any later training change must be selected solely by host macro validation HR@10.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a mask-safe recent-history usefulness probe to the corrected BERT4Rec diagnostic adapter. Compare deterministic training-prefix next-item predictions under controlled history interventions using the same independently trained model for each category.",
    "motivation": "Current diagnostics measure training-position separation and average embedding alignment, whereas feedback raises questions about specific recent interests. A controlled appended-mask probe can distinguish sequence dependence from dependence that helps held-out ranking, without inventing compatibility or intent labels.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), including metrics and metric_definitions. Access the actual network through view.model and retain item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data and review_data interfaces. Use only supplied training histories for prefix holdouts; exclude each chosen target and all subsequent interactions from its input. Preserve existing family-specific masked-position diagnostics, but flag or skip batches whose positive targets are visible rather than reporting misleading margins. Build diagnostic candidates deterministically from real item IDs, excluding the target and prefix items from negative sampling; reuse candidates and eligibility across interventions. These diagnostic candidates are separate from, and do not modify, the pinned host evaluator. For every branch, reserve space for the appended mask, left-pad, and gather the greatest nonpadding index, never nonpadding_count minus one. Report observation counts, undefined values and degenerate score variance explicitly. Use no unavailable metadata categories, ratings, reviews, ownership or simulator traits. Training remains preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, at most 200 epochs and 200 batches per epoch, single GPU. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'] and artifacts beneath the trial directory. No inference architecture change is required; if later introduced, update the embedded predictor writer and checkpoint loading together. Charge probe execution to the shared search budget.",
    "pseudocode": "run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    save view.model.training; set evaluation mode\n    under no_grad:\n        metrics = existing_mask_safe_training_diagnostics(train_loader)\n        examples = deterministic_prefix_holdouts(view.user_train, seed=42)\n        for user, prefix, target in examples:\n            candidates = fixed_real_item_candidates(prefix, target, seed=42)\n            branches = {baseline: prefix,\n                        recent_delete: remove_recent_k(prefix),\n                        older_delete: remove_oldest_k(prefix),\n                        recent_permute: permute_recent_window(prefix)}\n            add repeated random_k_deletion branches\n            for branch in branches:\n                sequence = left_pad(truncate(branch, L-1) + [MASK], L)\n                assert target_position_is_withheld_from_input_construction\n                features = view.log2feats(sequence)\n                index = maximum index where sequence[index] != PAD\n                logits = view.predict_score(features[:, index], candidates)\n                record target_rank, hit_at_10, top10_ids\n                record (target_logit - tenth_negative_logit) / std(logits)\n                flag undefined margins when variance is zero\n            record paired intervention-minus-baseline and intervention-minus-control effects\n        aggregate by category and baseline hit status\n        bootstrap paired users within category; average category effects equally\n        attach counts, uncertainty and precise metric_definitions\n    restore previous training mode\n    return {metrics, metric_definitions}\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    include measured metrics, definitions, counts and uncertainty\n    require evidence-linked findings\n    distinguish dependence, usefulness, missing evidence and host validation performance",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines BERT4Rec-specific inference safeguards with paired history controls and usefulness measurements.",
      "negative": "History interventions and paired ranking comparisons are established techniques; the contribution is an adapted diagnostic protocol."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides evidence for subsequent masking, sampling or regularization experiments while preserving one model per category.",
      "negative": "Training-prefix effects may not transfer to host validation and cannot establish compatibility or immediate purchase intent."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Reuses the existing ModelView and trained checkpoints without new model training or external data.",
      "negative": "Correct masking, positional controls, candidate reuse and paired uncertainty estimates require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec: Sequential Recommendation with Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes bidirectional masked-item training for sequential recommendation.",
        "Provides the family-specific foundation for appended-mask next-item inference."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the proposed intervention protocol or guarantee useful recency dependence.",
        "Requires careful target concealment when adapting diagnostic loaders."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sequential counterfactual recommendation study",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.03606",
      "contributions": [
        "Investigates minimal history edits that change recommendations, motivating controlled sequence interventions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Recommendation changes alone do not prove explanation quality or improved held-out ranking.",
        "The paired controls proposed here are a synthesis rather than a reported BERT4Rec protocol."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
